task(R7-SCOPE-INVERSION):
  title    = "R7 makes the stale rules table beat the corrected one — scope decision (L3, rmw3)"
  status   = done  // rmw3 2026-08-11 chose (B), matching [O]'s recommendation below
  priority = alta
  date     = "2026-08-10"
  owner    = "rmw3 [H] — hard-rule scope change is L3 per A5/D4, [O] cannot decide it"
  context:
    "RULES.md's header declares state.db's `rules` table the canonical machine form. Its own `next`
     section records that the 2026-08-03 rule compression (merged R1/R4, R6 status removed) was
     NEVER migrated there — only the git mirror carries it. R7 says state.db wins ties.
     ⟹ R7 instructs the agent to prefer the unmigrated table over the corrected file. The two
     documents that should agree are knowingly divergent, and the rule picks the wrong one.
     Measured live 2026-08-10 (`vsf-q rules`, retried past a `database is locked`): the table holds
     12 rules, ids 1..12, and diverges from the mirror in BOTH directions —
       it still carries  R2, R3 (merged into R1 by the 08-03 compression) and R12 (merged into R4);
       it is MISSING     R13 (visibility, ratified 2026-07-22) and R15 (VSL cross-agent exchange,
                         ratified 2026-08-03) — the table simply stops at 12.
     ⟹ R7 currently directs the agent to a table that lacks two ratified rules outright. This is not
     a formatting lag; it is missing normative content.
     R7's own justification is 'memory = point-in-time snapshot ; db = live'. That holds for the
     domains state.db OBSERVES — services, chains, topology, all written by vsf-state-populate from
     real measurement. Nothing observes `rules`: the table is hand-entered, so it is a point-in-time
     snapshot too. Applying an observation-derived precedence rule to a legislated domain is a
     category error, not a staleness bug."
  design:
    "Two mutually exclusive options. [O] recommends (B).
     (A) MIGRATE — rewrite state.db's `rules` table to match RULES.md's compressed structure, and
         keep R7 global. Cost: a second normative surface stays alive and must be re-synced by hand
         after every rule change, forever. The drift recurs the moment someone edits one and not the
         other — which is exactly how this inversion was born.
      (B) NARROW R7 — R7 applies to OBSERVED domains only (services, components, chains, topology,
          lxcs). Normative content (rules, axioms) is defined in git: docs/spec_revision/system/boot/ defines,
          RULES.md annotates, and state.db's `rules` table is demoted to a READ-ONLY MIRROR that
          never wins a tie. Requires: edit R7_state_db_wins in docs/spec_revision/system/boot/hard_rules.vsm (PROTOCOL_VERSION
          bump), and either drop the `rules` table or mark it mirror-only at the source.
     Whichever is chosen, RULES.md currently carries an explicit ? block at R7 saying the inversion
     is unresolved — remove it in the SAME commit that resolves this, per doc-sync discipline."
  gating   = "rmw3 decision — L3, cannot be taken autonomously (A5: approval(op, human) = required)"
  files    = [RULES.md, docs/spec_revision/system/boot/hard_rules.vsm]
  found_by = "boot audit 2026-08-10, layer L1 (RULES.md vs state.db rules table)"
  resolved_2026_08_11: "rmw3, verbatim: 'state.db se encarga de observar la
      realidad de n02... pero rules vive en el mundo del agente... las reglas
      deben venir de un solo archivo canónico, ya que las reglas aplican
      sobre procesos no deterministas, mientras que en n02 todas las
      operaciones son deterministas' — independently arrived at (B), sharper
      justification than [O]'s draft (the deterministic/non-deterministic
      domain split, not just 'observed vs hand-edited'). Implemented same
      session: vsl_hard_rules_v1.vsm R7_state_db_wins rewritten
      (PROTOCOL_VERSION 2->3, scoped to observed(state.db), rules domain
      carved out unconditionally to this file); RULES.md's R7 section and
      canon-header both updated, the unresolved-inversion warning removed;
      CLAUDE.md's instrument table marks state-canon-infra's rules domain
      informational-only. Table removal itself (not just scoping) dispatched
      to DS: task(R7-RULES-TABLE-CLEANUP-2026-08-11) — rmw3 explicit: 'una
      sola fuente de verdad es la política', don't leave a second, merely
      demoted, copy sitting there confusing the next reader."

