  what: "rmw3, on hearing about CLIMA-BRIDGE-NATS-CRASHLOOP-FIXED, asked the
      sharper question directly: why didn't WE see that either, and why no
      algedonic report? Answer surfaces a real, named architectural gap,
      not a one-off miss.",
  finding: "Two separate causes stack: (1) nothing scans the fleet for
      systemd-level flapping/health -- vsf-topology-reconcile (5min host
      timer) checks EXISTENCE (does state.db's declared service match what's
      running) not LIVENESS (is it actually healthy, or crash-looping every
      85s while technically 'running' at every sampled instant). (2) even if
      something did watch it, there is no wiring from local resilience
      mechanisms into the algedonic channel (vsf-s5-algedonic / ledger /
      g6-algedonic-sink) -- that channel only carries what VSF app code
      explicitly chooses to emit as phi_up pain events. systemd's own
      Restart=always + WatchdogSec is a purely local, mechanical self-healing
      loop with zero upward signal by construction.",
  pattern_this_session_x3: [
    "PSI (cpu.pressure/io.pressure) accumulates real kernel data, zero consumers -- @ref THERMAL-RESOURCE-GOVERNANCE-AMENDMENT thread 4",
    "vsf-thermal-watchdog.py (shipped this session) explicitly does NOT feed Omega/S3/S4/S5 by design -- journal+state-file only",
    "vsf-s4-clima-bridge crash-looped 925x/~22h with zero upward signal -- CLIMA-BRIDGE-NATS-CRASHLOOP-FIXED",
  ],
  framing: "Not three unrelated gaps -- one structural pattern: local
      resilience (watchdogs, restart policies, kernel pressure counters) and
      upward observability (algedonic/phi_up) are two disconnected systems.
      Building more local resilience mechanisms without wiring any of them
      up doesn't reduce this risk, it just adds more silent islands.",
  candidate_shape_for_dispatch: "A minimal fleet health-to-algedonic bridge:
      periodic (host-level, alongside vsf-topology-reconcile) scan of
      NRestarts/ActiveState across declared services in state.db: if a
      service's NRestarts crosses a threshold in a window (e.g. >5 in
      10min), or ActiveState sits in 'activating'/'failed' past a grace
      period, emit ONE algedonic-channel event (not per-restart spam). Same
      shape would also give PSI and the thermal watchdog a path upward if/
      when THERMAL-RESOURCE-GOVERNANCE-AMENDMENT's S3-bargain design lands --
      this task could be the shared plumbing both threads need, done once.",
  next: "queued, no urgency per the same 'document now, attack when a work
      window opens' spirit as THERMAL-RESOURCE-GOVERNANCE-AMENDMENT -- related
      but kept as its own task since the fix (a generic health-to-algedonic
      bridge) is infrastructure-layer, not resource-governance-specific, and
      shouldn't get scoped-down to just thermal/cgroup concerns.",
}

